QUESTION-1
1. Write a C program for Caesar cipher involves replacing each letter of the alphabet with the letter
standing k places further down the alphabet, for k in the range 1 through 25.

AIM
To write a C program to implement the Caesar cipher, which replaces each letter of the alphabet with the letter standing k places further down the alphabet, where k is in the range 1 to 25.
PROCEDURE
Start the program.
Read the plaintext message from the user.
Read the shift value k from 1 to 25.
For each character in the message:
If it is an uppercase letter, shift it by k positions.
If it is a lowercase letter, shift it by k positions.
Keep spaces and special characters unchanged.
If the shift goes beyond Z or z, wrap around to the beginning of the alphabet.
Display the encrypted ciphertext.
Stop the program.
CODE:
#include <stdio.h>
#include <string.h>
int main()
{
    char text[100];
    int k, i;
    printf("Enter the plaintext: ");
    fgets(text, sizeof(text), stdin);
    printf("Enter the shift value (1-25): ");
    scanf("%d", &k);
    if (k < 1 || k > 25)
    {
        printf("Invalid shift value! Enter a value between 1 and 25.\n");
        return 0;
    }
    for (i = 0; text[i] != '\0'; i++)
    {
        if (text[i] >= 'A' && text[i] <= 'Z')
        {
            text[i] = ((text[i] - 'A' + k) % 26) + 'A';
        }
        else if (text[i] >= 'a' && text[i] <= 'z')
        {
            text[i] = ((text[i] - 'a' + k) % 26) + 'a';
        }
    }
    printf("Encrypted text: %s", text);
    return 0;
}
INPUT:
Enter the plaintext: MEET AT NOON
Enter the shift value (1-25): 4
OUTPUT:
Encrypted text: QIIX EX RSSR



RESULT
Thus, the Caesar cipher was successfully implemented in C, and the plaintext was encrypted by shifting each alphabet character by the specified value k.




